# Events of the two arbiter facets that the subgraph deliberately does not index.
#
# Checked by: ./script/check-subgraph-arbiter-events.sh
# Rule:       every event declared by ArbiterAccountabilityFacet or
#             ArbiterRegistryFacet is either handled by the subgraph or written
#             down here with a reason. An event in neither place turns the gate
#             red.
# Rule 2:     every OTHER .sol under src/ is written off at the bottom of this
#             file, with a reason and a pinned count of the events it declares.
#             A file in neither place turns the gate red too — see the second
#             header down there for what that costs and why it is worth it.
#
# A third list lives in a file of its own, script/arbiter-mistake-paths.allow:
# every way an arbiter can be booked a judicial mistake, with the log each one
# leaves. It is not a list of exceptions, which is why it is not in here.
#
# THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE THE GATE USED TO BE SILENT OVER HALF ITS AREA.
# Coverage was enforced over ArbiterAccountabilityFacet only, on the argument
# that the registry emits thirty-odd events with nothing to do with
# accountability. The cost of that argument was measured on 21 August 2026:
# RemovalProposedByChain and ChainAccusationCleared — the chain accusing an
# arbiter in its own name, and the withdrawal of that accusation — sat
# unindexed from 18 to 21 August with every gate in the repository green. The
# only thing that remembered them was a comment. A reader of the feed in those
# three days saw a removal with no accusation, no pause and no answer behind
# it.
#
# So the tax is paid instead: twenty-six entries below, most of them one line,
# and the next event added to either facet goes red until somebody decides
# which half it belongs in.
#
# FORMAT. A record is a header line
#
#     === <Facet> :: <EventName> ===
#
# followed by a line that begins literally with
#
#     not indexed, because:
#
# The reason continues over following lines until the next header. A record
# without that line makes the gate treat the file as damaged and refuse — the
# same shape, and the same deliberate inconvenience, as
# script/gasless-sender.allow: an exception cannot be taken without saying out
# loud why.
#
# ⚠️ NOTHING IS PINNED HERE, unlike the `occurrences:` counts in
# gasless-sender.allow, and that is a decision rather than an omission. There,
# the bug class is a NEW raw msg.sender appearing inside a body that was already
# allowed, so the count is what catches it. Here the bug class is a new EVENT
# nobody indexed, and coverage catches that by itself. What is therefore NOT
# caught: an event listed below changing its parameters or its `indexed` marks.
# Nothing reads it, so nothing breaks — and the day one of these moves into the
# feed it leaves this file, at which point the parameter-for-parameter
# comparison in the gate starts holding it against solc.
#
# ⚠️ AND THE LIST IS ABOUT THIS FEED, NOT ABOUT IMPORTANCE. Several entries
# below are read by the frontend or by the relayer through their own ABIs;
# "not indexed" means "no accountability record needs it", nothing more.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALREADY IN THE FEED UNDER ANOTHER NAME
#
# Two events whose information is indexed — through a different log that says
# strictly more.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: ArbiterAdded ===
not indexed, because: ArbiterSeated is emitted beside it at both entry doors and
  carries the same seating plus the presser and the self-service flag. The bare
  event is kept in the contract for the subgraph that is LIVE on chain (v2.3.0
  reads it), and its declaration says so. Indexing both would put one seating in
  the feed twice.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: ArbiterApplied ===
not indexed, because: the self-service half of the same seating.
  applyAsArbiter emits ArbiterSeated(caller, address(0), true) in the next line,
  and the zero presser with selfService true is the whole of what this event
  says.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DISPUTE, NOT THE ARBITER'S STANDING
#
# The life of one dispute: who took it, who let it go, what was frozen, what
# was presented. None of it changes what the corps may be held to account for.
# Several have live readers elsewhere — the frontend counts arbiter turns off
# DisputeClaimed, the relayer pushes on it — through their own ABI copies.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DisputeClaimCommitted ===
not indexed, because: the commit half of the commit-reveal that stops arbiters
  racing for a dispute. It names a commitment hash, not a dispute — there is
  nothing an accountability record could hang off it until the reveal.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DisputeClaimed ===
not indexed, because: assignment, not accountability. The arbiter of a dispute
  reaches the feed when it matters — VerdictSubmitted names him, and that is
  the point where a mistake becomes possible at all.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DisputeReleased ===
not indexed, because: an arbiter letting a dispute go costs him nothing and is
  not a judicial mistake. The mistake case is the one he does NOT release and
  runs out the clock on, which is ArbiterTimeoutRecorded.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DisputeNoResponseRecorded ===
not indexed, because: the chain-as-witness work of 15 August. It records that a
  PARTY did not answer the arbiter, which is evidence inside a dispute and never
  a mark against the arbiter.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: PresentationDigestRecorded ===
not indexed, because: the other half of the same work — a party fixing the
  digest of what it showed the arbiter. Evidence within a dispute; it says
  nothing about the arbiter's standing.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: ArbiterChatKeySet ===
not indexed, because: the arbiter publishing or rotating the keys a party
  encrypts a presentation to. It has a live reader — the frontend watches it so
  a presentation can be repeated when the arbiter changes device — and that
  reader takes it straight off the chain through its own ABI. Nothing about a
  key is conduct.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: VerdictFrozen ===
not indexed, because: a freeze suspends finalization and touches neither the
  mistake streak nor the counters. The Verdict entity holds what the series
  needs — submitted, overturned, finalized — and a freeze changes none of the
  three.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: VerdictUnfrozen ===
not indexed, because: the reverse of the freeze, and silent about the arbiter
  for the same reason.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: StuckVerdictAutoCleared ===
not indexed, because: it throws away a verdict nobody finalized so the dispute
  can be claimed again — no mistake is booked and no counter moves.
  ⚠️ NAMED RATHER THAN WAVED THROUGH: the Verdict entity is keyed by the
  agreement, so a cleared verdict that is never resubmitted leaves a stale
  record with finalized false. Nothing reads it — the series only ever asks
  about verdicts that reach an overturn or a finalization — and a resubmission
  overwrites it whole in handleVerdictSubmitted. Index this the day something
  starts reading "the standing verdict" off that entity.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: AppealRaised ===
not indexed, because: raising an appeal is the losing party's move, not the
  arbiter's. What the series needs is how it ended, and AppealResolved is
  indexed for exactly that.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: AppealVoteCast ===
not indexed, because: it names the VOTER, never the arbiter being judged — the
  gap that made the appeal path unrecoverable until AppealResolved was paired
  with the verdict's own arbiter. A record of who voted how is governance
  history and would be a feed of its own.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MONEY
#
# The arbiter payment ledger and the bounty on a dispute. Amounts, never
# conduct.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: ArbiterRewarded ===
not indexed, because: payment for work done. An accountability record that
  counted rewards would be answering a different question.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: ArbiterRewardWithdrawn ===
not indexed, because: the arbiter moving his own earned balance out.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DisputeFeeCredited ===
not indexed, because: the split of a dispute fee between the arbiter and the
  treasury.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DisputeBountyFunded ===
not indexed, because: a party paying to get a dispute looked at.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DisputeBountyRefunded ===
not indexed, because: that payment going back.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DisputeBountyRefundable ===
not indexed, because: the same money becoming claimable when a push failed.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DisputeBountyWithdrawn ===
not indexed, because: the payer collecting it.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: VaultFunded ===
not indexed, because: the bank arbiters are paid out of, topped up.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: TreasurySlicePushed ===
not indexed, because: the treasury's share of a fee leaving the diamond.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: ArbiterFloorUpdated ===
not indexed, because: an economic parameter of the corps — what an arbiter is
  paid — set by the owner. Not conduct, and not a thing anyone is answerable
  for.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHO GOVERNS
#
# The seats above the corps. Adjacent to accountability and deliberately left
# out for now; the note on the first entry says what would change that.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: ChiefArbiterSet ===
not indexed, because: it names the director's seat, and the director is the one
  who may PROPOSE removals. That makes it the closest thing in this section to
  accountability, and it stays out only because no record needs it: every
  proposal already carries its author in `by`, so the feed answers "who accused
  him" without knowing who held the seat that day. Index it the day a reader
  asks the other question — "who could have accused him then" — which is a
  question about the office rather than the act.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DAOActivated ===
not indexed, because: the handover of the corps to the DAO. A one-off
  governance switch, readable from the chain directly (isDaoActive), with no
  per-arbiter record to attach to.

=== ArbiterRegistryFacet :: DAOAddressSet ===
not indexed, because: the address of that DAO being set, for the same reason.


# ===========================================================================
#  THE OTHER KIND OF RECORD: FILES, NOT EVENTS
#
#  Every .sol under src/ is either read by this gate — today that is the two
#  arbiter facets — or written off below with a reason and a PINNED count of
#  the events it declares.
#
#  WHY THE AREA IS DEFINED BY SUBTRACTION. Until 21 August 2026 the gate knew
#  two contracts by name and looked nowhere else. A reviewer measured what that
#  costs: he declared an arbiter event in ReputationFacet.sol and the gate
#  stayed green. It is the same hole one floor up from the one the event list
#  closed — the old rule read one facet out of two, the new one read two
#  contracts out of sixteen. Defining the area as "everything, minus what is
#  written off" is the only shape where a file nobody thought about fails
#  loudly, and it is the shape script/gasless-sender.allow already uses for
#  exactly this reason.
#
#  WHAT THE PIN IS FOR. Without `events: <n>`, writing a file off once writes
#  off everything ever added to it, and the reviewer's measurement goes green
#  again the moment somebody puts an arbiter event in a written-off file. With
#  it, the count moves and the gate stops. Raising a pin is a decision: it says
#  "I looked at the new event and it is not about arbiter accountability".
#
#  ⚠️ ZERO IS A LEGAL VALUE and says something worth saying: this file declares
#  no events at all, and if it grows one, somebody will look.
# ===========================================================================

=== out of scope :: src/Agreement.sol ===
no accountability events, because: the escrow itself. Its events are about ONE
  deal — funded, activated, released, disputed — and they are already indexed,
  by the AgreementContract template in subgraph.yaml rather than by the diamond
  data source this gate guards.
  ⚠️ AND IT IS THE MOST INSTRUCTIVE ENTRY IN THIS FILE. Agreement declares
  ArbiterTimedOut(address indexed client, uint256), whose NAME says arbiter and
  whose first field is the client. That event is the reason the timeout path
  looked observable for two days and was not: reading the arbiter out of it is
  impossible, because the arbiter is not in it. A file being written off here
  means "no accountability event lives here", never "nothing here mentions
  arbiters".
events: 21

=== out of scope :: src/facets/JobBoardFacet.sol ===
no accountability events, because: the job board. Posting, applying, accepting,
  cancelling — the marketplace, indexed by handlers of its own in
  subgraph/src/diamond.ts, and touching no arbiter.
events: 7

=== out of scope :: src/facets/ServiceBoardFacet.sol ===
no accountability events, because: the service board, the executor-side twin of
  the job board and out of scope for the same reason.
events: 11

=== out of scope :: src/FactoryFacet.sol ===
no accountability events, because: deal creation and the protocol's keys —
  fee recipient, forwarder, deployer. It names an arbiter when a deal is
  deployed, and that is an assignment, never conduct.
events: 10

=== out of scope :: src/RegistryFacet.sol ===
no accountability events, because: the list of deployed agreements and their
  status. It is the registry of DEALS; the registry of arbiters is the facet
  this gate reads, and the two share a name and nothing else.
events: 3

=== out of scope :: src/facets/ReputationFacet.sol ===
no accountability events, because: XP. An arbiter loses XP when a verdict of his
  is overturned — _slashArbiterXP writes this facet's storage — but the writing
  is done FROM the registry, and the mark of the mistake is the registry's event.
  What lives here is a number, not a record of conduct.
  ⚠️ THIS IS THE FILE THE REVIEWER USED to show the gate was blind to everything
  outside its two-name list. An arbiter event declared here now moves the pin
  below and stops the gate.
events: 2

=== out of scope :: src/Treasury.sol ===
no accountability events, because: the protocol's own money — the ladder that
  splits income between the arbiter vault, the foundation and the reserve. It
  funds the corps and judges nobody.
events: 4

=== out of scope :: src/JobReceiptFacet.sol ===
no accountability events, because: the soulbound receipt minted when a job is
  posted. NFT bookkeeping.
events: 4

=== out of scope :: src/DiamondProxy.sol ===
no accountability events, because: the proxy, its ownership and the cut. Its
  events are about which facet answers which selector — the plumbing under every
  other file here, arbiter facets included.
events: 5

=== out of scope :: src/MinimalForwarder.sol ===
no accountability events, because: the ERC-2771 forwarder, a separate deployed
  contract that is not part of the diamond at all. It knows who paid for gas and
  nothing else.
events: 1

=== out of scope :: src/AgreementDeployer.sol ===
no accountability events, because: it clones the Agreement implementation and
  declares no events at all. The zero below is the assertion.
events: 0

=== out of scope :: src/SVGRenderer.sol ===
no accountability events, because: on-chain SVG for the receipt NFT. Pure
  rendering, no events.
events: 0

=== out of scope :: src/facets/DealMetadataFacet.sol ===
no accountability events, because: on-chain metadata for the deal NFT. Rendering
  again, and no events.
events: 0

=== out of scope :: src/facets/IFactory.sol ===
no accountability events, because: an interface. It declares functions for other
  contracts to call and no events.
events: 0
